<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Indo-Scythians</h1><p class="st-meta">110 BCE – 30 CE (approx.) · capital Taxila</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Saka nomads from Central Asia who took over north-western India from the Greeks and founded lines of satraps that ruled for centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/saka">Sakas</a>, Scythian nomads of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, were driven south by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> in the 2nd century BCE. They passed through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arachosia">Arachosia</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> valley. Around 85 BCE their king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maues">Maues</a> established himself in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-gandhara">Gandhara</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a> from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom</a>, founding a Saka kingdom in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Indo-Scythian kings called themselves <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/negus-nagast">king of kings</a> and copied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek</a> coinage, with Greek and Kharoshthi legends. They ruled through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satraps</a>, provincial governors who often became hereditary rulers. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azes-i">Azes I</a>, whose era beginning in 57 BCE was used in inscriptions, the kingdom spread over <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-gandhara">Gandhara</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>. They patronised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, as the Bimaran reliquary and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura-lion-capital">Mathura Lion Capital</a> show, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila's</a> city at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirkap">Sirkap</a> flourished.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the early 1st century the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/indo-parthian-kingdom">Indo-Parthian Kingdom</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gondophares">Gondophares</a> took over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-gandhara">Gandhara</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/saka">Saka</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satraps</a> survived elsewhere, among them the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-satraps">Northern Satraps</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-satraps">Western Satraps</a>, who ruled western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> until about 415.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/indo-scythians.jpg" alt="Indo-Scythians" width="320" height="296"><figcaption>Image: Map created from DEMIS Mapserver, which are public domain. Koba-chan.Contouring and city names: DLommes · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Indo-Scythians.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>110 BCE – 30 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Taxila</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Maues, Azes I, Azilises</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Hinduism, Iranian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Saka, Gandhari Prakrit, Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, <a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Indo-Scythian Kingdom, Indo-Sakas, Saka kingdom of Taxila</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-40&amp;focus=indo-scythians" aria-label="Indo-Scythians on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6628 1135 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Indo-Scythians lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6948 1391L6804 1292M6948 1391L6858 1210M6948 1391L6815 1178M6948 1391L7040 1426"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6804 1292h0M6858 1210h0M6815 1178h0M7040 1426h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6948 1391h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6948 1391h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-40&amp;focus=indo-scythians">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
